"""\n================================================================================\n                    RECURSION - COMPLETE LEARNING GUIDE\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IS RECURSION?\n------------------\nRecursion is a programming technique where a function calls itself to solve a\nproblem by breaking it down into smaller, similar subproblems.\n\nA recursive function has two main components:\n1. BASE CASE: Condition that stops the recursion\n2. RECURSIVE CASE: Function calls itself with modified parameters\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        BASIC STRUCTURE\n================================================================================\n\nGeneral Pattern:\n\ndef recursive_function(parameters):\n    # BASE CASE - Stops recursion\n    if base_condition:\n        return base_value\n    \n    # RECURSIVE CASE - Calls itself\n    # Do some work\n    result = recursive_function(modified_parameters)\n    # Do some more work\n    return result\n\n\nExample: Factorial\n\ndef factorial(n):\n    # Base case\n    if n == 0 or n == 1:\n        return 1\n    \n    # Recursive case\n    return n * factorial(n - 1)\n\nCall Stack for factorial(4):\n    factorial(4)\n    └─→ 4 * factorial(3)\n        └─→ 3 * factorial(2)\n            └─→ 2 * factorial(1)\n                └─→ 1  (base case)\n            ← 2 * 1 = 2\n        ← 3 * 2 = 6\n    ← 4 * 6 = 24\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        RECURSION vs ITERATION\n================================================================================\n\nRecursive Factorial:\n    def factorial_recursive(n):\n        if n <= 1:\n            return 1\n        return n * factorial_recursive(n - 1)\n\nIterative Factorial:\n    def factorial_iterative(n):\n        result = 1\n        for i in range(2, n + 1):\n            result *= i\n        return result\n\nComparison:\n\nFeature          | Recursion           | Iteration\n-----------------|---------------------|------------------\nReadability      | Often cleaner       | Can be complex\nMemory           | Uses call stack     | Uses variables\nPerformance      | Slower (overhead)   | Faster\nProblem types    | Tree/graph problems | Linear problems\nStack overflow   | Possible            | No\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        TYPES OF RECURSION\n================================================================================\n\n1. DIRECT RECURSION\n   Function calls itself directly.\n   \n   def func():\n       func()  # Direct call\n\n\n2. INDIRECT RECURSION\n   Function A calls B, B calls A.\n   \n   def funcA():\n       funcB()\n   \n   def funcB():\n       funcA()\n\n\n3. TAIL RECURSION\n   Recursive call is the last operation.\n   \n   def tail_recursive(n, accumulator=0):\n       if n == 0:\n           return accumulator\n       return tail_recursive(n - 1, accumulator + n)\n   \n   ✓ Can be optimized by compiler\n   ✓ No work after recursive call\n\n\n4. HEAD RECURSION\n   Recursive call is the first operation.\n   \n   def head_recursive(n):\n       if n > 0:\n           head_recursive(n - 1)\n           print(n)\n\n\n5. TREE RECURSION\n   Function calls itself multiple times.\n   \n   def fibonacci(n):\n       if n <= 1:\n           return n\n       return fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2)\n   \n   Call tree for fib(4):\n                fib(4)\n              /        \\\n          fib(3)      fib(2)\n         /     \\      /     \\\n      fib(2) fib(1) fib(1) fib(0)\n      /   \\\n   fib(1) fib(0)\n\n\n6. NESTED RECURSION\n   Recursive call takes another recursive call as argument.\n   \n   def ackermann(m, n):\n       if m == 0:\n           return n + 1\n       if n == 0:\n           return ackermann(m - 1, 1)\n       return ackermann(m - 1, ackermann(m, n - 1))\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        RECURSION PATTERNS\n================================================================================\n\n1. DECREASING AND CONQUERING\n   Reduce problem size by constant amount.\n   \n   def print_n(n):\n       if n > 0:\n           print(n)\n           print_n(n - 1)\n\n\n2. DIVIDE AND CONQUER\n   Split problem into independent subproblems.\n   \n   def merge_sort(arr):\n       if len(arr) <= 1:\n           return arr\n       \n       mid = len(arr) // 2\n       left = merge_sort(arr[:mid])\n       right = merge_sort(arr[mid:])\n       return merge(left, right)\n\n\n3. TWO POINTER RECURSION\n   Use two pointers moving towards each other.\n   \n   def reverse_array(arr, left, right):\n       if left >= right:\n           return\n       arr[left], arr[right] = arr[right], arr[left]\n       reverse_array(arr, left + 1, right - 1)\n\n\n4. BACKTRACKING\n   Try all possibilities, backtrack if invalid.\n   \n   def generate_strings(n, current=\"\"):\n       if len(current) == n:\n           print(current)\n           return\n       generate_strings(n, current + \"0\")\n       generate_strings(n, current + \"1\")\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        CALL STACK & MEMORY\n================================================================================\n\nEach recursive call creates a new stack frame containing:\n- Function parameters\n- Local variables\n- Return address\n\nExample: factorial(3)\n\nStack Frames:\n┌─────────────────┐\n│ factorial(3)    │  ← Current\n│ n = 3           │\n├─────────────────┤\n│ factorial(2)    │\n│ n = 2           │\n├─────────────────┤\n│ factorial(1)    │\n│ n = 1           │\n└─────────────────┘\n\nMemory Usage: O(n) where n is recursion depth\nStack Overflow occurs if depth is too large!\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        RECURSION TREE METHOD\n================================================================================\n\nUsed to analyze time complexity.\n\nExample: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n (Merge Sort)\n\n              n              → Work at this level: n\n            /   \\\n          n/2   n/2          → Work at this level: n\n         /  \\  /  \\\n       n/4 n/4 n/4 n/4      → Work at this level: n\n       ...\n\nHeight: log n\nWork per level: n\nTotal work: n * log n = O(n log n)\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        MASTER THEOREM\n================================================================================\n\nFor recurrences: T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n)\n\nCase 1: If f(n) = O(n^(log_b(a) - ε))\n        Then T(n) = Θ(n^log_b(a))\n\nCase 2: If f(n) = Θ(n^log_b(a))\n        Then T(n) = Θ(n^log_b(a) * log n)\n\nCase 3: If f(n) = Ω(n^(log_b(a) + ε))\n        Then T(n) = Θ(f(n))\n\nExamples:\n- Merge Sort: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n  → O(n log n)\n- Binary Search: T(n) = T(n/2) + 1  → O(log n)\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        COMMON RECURSIVE PROBLEMS\n================================================================================\n\n1. MATHEMATICAL\n   - Factorial\n   - Fibonacci\n   - Power(x, n)\n   - GCD (Greatest Common Divisor)\n   - Sum of digits\n\n2. ARRAY/STRING\n   - Reverse array\n   - Check palindrome\n   - Binary search\n   - Generate subsequences\n   - Print all permutations\n\n3. TREE\n   - Tree traversals (inorder, preorder, postorder)\n   - Height of tree\n   - Check if BST\n   - Count nodes\n\n4. BACKTRACKING\n   - N-Queens problem\n   - Sudoku solver\n   - Generate parentheses\n   - Subset sum\n   - Combinations\n\n5. DIVIDE AND CONQUER\n   - Merge sort\n   - Quick sort\n   - Closest pair of points\n   - Largest rectangle\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        OPTIMIZATION TECHNIQUES\n================================================================================\n\n1. MEMOIZATION (Top-Down DP)\n   Store computed results to avoid recomputation.\n   \n   # Without memoization: O(2^n)\n   def fib(n):\n       if n <= 1:\n           return n\n       return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)\n   \n   # With memoization: O(n)\n   def fib_memo(n, memo={}):\n       if n in memo:\n           return memo[n]\n       if n <= 1:\n           return n\n       memo[n] = fib_memo(n-1, memo) + fib_memo(n-2, memo)\n       return memo[n]\n\n\n2. TAIL CALL OPTIMIZATION\n   Convert to iterative form.\n   \n   # Tail recursive\n   def factorial_tail(n, acc=1):\n       if n == 0:\n           return acc\n       return factorial_tail(n-1, n*acc)\n\n\n3. CONVERT TO ITERATION\n   Use stack/queue explicitly.\n   \n   # Recursive\n   def factorial_rec(n):\n       if n <= 1:\n           return 1\n       return n * factorial_rec(n-1)\n   \n   # Iterative\n   def factorial_iter(n):\n       result = 1\n       for i in range(2, n+1):\n           result *= i\n       return result\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        ADVANTAGES & DISADVANTAGES\n================================================================================\n\nADVANTAGES:\n✓ Code is cleaner and easier to understand\n✓ Natural for tree/graph problems\n✓ Reduces time complexity with DP\n✓ Elegant solution for divide-and-conquer\n✓ Solves problems hard to do iteratively\n\nDISADVANTAGES:\n✗ Uses more memory (call stack)\n✗ Slower than iteration (function call overhead)\n✗ Risk of stack overflow\n✗ Harder to debug\n✗ Not all languages optimize tail recursion\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        IMPORTANT TIPS\n================================================================================\n\n1. Always define a clear base case\n2. Ensure progress toward base case\n3. Trust the recursion (assume smaller problem is solved)\n4. Draw recursion tree for understanding\n5. Use memoization for overlapping subproblems\n6. Consider converting to iteration for simple cases\n7. Watch out for stack overflow with deep recursion\n8. Python has recursion limit (usually 1000)\n   - Can increase: sys.setrecursionlimit()\n9. Test with small inputs first\n10. Trace through code with small example\n\n\n================================================================================\n                        PRACTICE PROBLEMS\n================================================================================\n\nBEGINNER:\n1. Print n numbers\n2. Sum of first n numbers\n3. Factorial\n4. Fibonacci\n5. Power(x, n)\n6. Print array\n7. Reverse array\n\nINTERMEDIATE:\n1. Binary search\n2. Tower of Hanoi\n3. Generate all subsets\n4. Sum of digits\n5. Check palindrome\n6. Count ways to climb stairs\n7. Generate balanced parentheses\n\nADVANCED:\n1. N-Queens problem\n2. Sudoku solver\n3. Word break\n4. Combinations and permutations\n5. Path sum in tree\n6. Longest increasing subsequence\n7. Edit distance\n\n================================================================================\n"""